  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the ripple effect of kindness?",
      "a": "It is the idea that one kind act spreads: the person helped, and people who see it, become more likely to be kind to others, so kindness travels outward like ripples in a pond.",
      "asks": [
        "Does kindness spread?",
        "Kindness ripple effect meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does helping someone matter so much?",
      "a": "A kind act helps the person in the moment and also lifts their mood, which makes them more likely to help others. It is the pebble that starts the ripples.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about you help Ana",
        "Do small acts of kindness matter?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does pay it forward mean?",
      "a": "Paying it forward means repaying a kindness by doing something kind for someone else, rather than for the person who helped you. That way kindness keeps moving.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about Ana helps Ben",
        "Pay it forward meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does watching kindness make people kinder?",
      "a": "Yes. Studies show that seeing someone act kindly can inspire watchers to be kinder too. Psychologists call the warm, inspired feeling elevation.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about Leo saw you",
        "Can kindness be contagious?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How far can kindness spread?",
      "a": "A 2010 study by Fowler and Christakis found cooperative behaviour spread up to three steps through a network: to people your friends' friends' friends meet.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about three steps away",
        "Who studied the spread of kindness?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Fowler and Christakis (2010, PNAS) found that cooperative behaviour in a giving game cascaded up to three degrees of separation.",
    "The phrase \"pay it forward\" was made popular by Catherine Ryan Hyde's 1999 novel \"Pay It Forward\"."
  ],
